{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "02", "section": "02", "chapter_title": "Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming", "title": "Two-AI Critique Workflow", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting two-ai critique workflow to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming", "cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/02/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_calibration"]}, "content": "# Two-AI Critique Workflow\n\n![Two-AI Critique Workflow textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-02-two-ai-critique-workflow.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAsk one AI tool to critique your resume and it will sound authoritative: it returns confident edits in fluent prose, and the easy reflex is to accept all of them. The problem is that a single tool gives you no way to tell a good suggestion from a fluent-sounding bad one. The two-AI workflow fixes this by running the same draft through two different tools (for example, ChatGPT and Microsoft Copilot, or Gemini) and reading the points where they disagree. Disagreement is the signal: when both tools say the same thing, it is probably safe; when they conflict, the decision is yours, and that is exactly where your judgment shows.\n\nThis matters because the workplace will not hand you one trusted source either. Learning to triangulate, to compare two automated opinions against the real requirement, is a transferable habit. The deliverable, a critique log marking each suggestion accepted, modified, or rejected with a reason, is proof that you used the tools as advisors rather than as authors.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nDevon runs a cover-letter paragraph through two AI assistants. The first says \"cut this sentence, it is redundant.\" The second says \"expand this sentence, it is your strongest point.\" Both sound certain. Devon's first instinct is to pick whichever reply came first, but that would let the tool, not Devon, make the call. Instead Devon goes back to the job posting, which explicitly asks for \"examples of measurable impact,\" and realizes the sentence in question is the only place a number appears. The second tool was right, but only because the posting settled it.\n\nThe case shows the core lesson: the tools cannot resolve their own disagreement, because neither one read the job posting or knows your history. The conflict is not a malfunction; it is the moment your reasoning has to enter, anchored to evidence the tools never saw.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe two-AI workflow is a structured comparison, not a vote. You send identical input to two independent tools, capture each critique verbatim, and then sort the results into three buckets: **agreement** (both flag the same issue, usually safe to act on), **conflict** (they contradict each other, requires your judgment), and **unique** (only one raised it, worth a deliberate decision). The value is concentrated in the conflict bucket, because that is where a single tool would have quietly steered you.\n\nThe reason two tools help is that large language models are not interchangeable: they are trained on different data, tuned with different guidelines, and prompted through different interfaces, so they fail in different places. One conflict resolution principle governs the whole process:\n\n| Situation | Who decides | On what basis |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tools agree | You confirm | Check it still fits your evidence |\n| Tools conflict | You break the tie | The job posting and your real record |\n| Tool invents a fact | You reject | Nothing in your record supports it |\n\nThe tie-breaker is never \"the tool I like better.\" It is always an external anchor: the posting, the rubric, or a fact you can defend. A polished merge of two AI replies that ignores the posting is worse than a plain draft that answers it.\n\n## Worked Example: Running One Cover-Letter Line Through Two Tools\n\nHere is the opening line of a student's cover letter:\n\n```\nI am writing to express my strong interest in the marketing internship\nat your company, where I believe I would be a great fit.\n```\n\n**Step 1. Send the same line to two different tools and capture their critiques verbatim.**\n\n| Tool | Critique |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tool A | \"Too generic. Name the company and a specific reason you want this role.\" |\n| Tool B | \"Clear and professional. No change needed.\" |\n\n**Step 2. Notice the conflict.** A says revise, B says leave it. The tools cannot resolve this for you, because neither one read the job posting. You did.\n\n**Step 3. Apply your own criterion.** The posting names a specific product line and asks for \"candidates excited about social-first campaigns.\" That detail tips the decision toward Tool A.\n\n**Step 4. Make the concrete change.**\n\n```\nI am applying for the Social Media Marketing Internship at Brightline\nOutdoors because your recent shift to short-form video campaigns matches\nthe Instagram work I did for my campus club.\n```\n\n**Step 5. Write the log entry that proves the judgment.**\n\n```\nLine 1. Tool A: \"too generic, name the company + reason.\" Tool B: \"no change needed.\"\nDecision: followed Tool A; rejected Tool B because the posting asks for a\nspecific reason. Edit: added company name + the social-first detail from the posting.\n```\n\nThe deliverable is not just the better line. It is the log row showing you used the disagreement, not a coin flip, to decide.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Resolve a two-tool conflict on one cover-letter line\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Tool A says \\\"too generic, name the company and a reason.\\\" Tool B says \\\"clear and professional, no change needed.\\\" What bucket is this?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Agreement\",\"Conflict\",\"Unique\",\"Fabrication\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Conflict. The tools contradict each other, so neither can settle it. This is exactly where your judgment has to enter.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Neither tool read the job posting. How do you break the tie?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Pick whichever reply arrived first\",\"Use an external anchor: reread the posting and let its stated requirement decide\",\"Pick the tool you usually trust\",\"Average the two suggestions\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The tie-breaker is never the tool you like. The posting names a product line and asks for \\\"social-first\\\" candidates, which tips the decision toward Tool A.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Revised line: \\\"I am applying for the Social Media Marketing Internship at Brightline Outdoors because your recent shift to short-form video campaigns matches the Instagram work I did for my campus club.\\\"\"},{\"prompt\":\"What is the actual deliverable for this line?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The polished sentence alone\",\"The log row: A vs B, which source decided, accepted/rejected with reason\",\"A screenshot of both tools\",\"An email to the recruiter\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The log is the evidence of reasoning. It records that you followed A, rejected B, and that the posting (not a coin flip) broke the tie.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWork the buckets in order, and log a reason for every suggestion you touch.\n\n1. **Act on agreement first.** Where both tools flag the same issue, fix it; this is the low-risk gain.\n2. **Resolve conflicts with an external anchor.** Reread the job posting or rubric and let it break the tie. Record which source decided it.\n3. **Decide the unique calls deliberately.** A suggestion only one tool raised still needs a yes or no with a reason, not a shrug.\n\nMark each entry accepted, modified, or rejected. \"Modified\" is common and honest: you can take the idea (add a metric) while rewriting the wording in your own voice. If your log has only \"accepted\" entries, you probably stopped reading critically.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Act on agreement first: fix what both tools flag (low-risk gain)\",\"Resolve conflicts with an external anchor: reread the posting/rubric and record which source decided\",\"Decide each unique call deliberately with a yes/no and a reason\",\"Mark every touched suggestion accepted, modified, or rejected in the log\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Both tools independently say \\\"this sentence is wordy, tighten it.\\\" Which bucket is that, and what do you do?\" explanation=\"When both tools flag the same issue it is agreement, the low-risk bucket you act on first, after confirming the tighter version still fits your evidence. Agreement lowers risk; it does not need a tie-break.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Agreement; act on it first after confirming it still fits your evidence\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Conflict; reread the posting to break the tie\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Unique; decide deliberately with a reason\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Fabrication; reject it outright\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nSome students treat the two tools as a majority vote, picking whatever two replies have in common and discarding the rest. But two tools agreeing can both be wrong, especially when both miss the posting's actual requirement. Agreement lowers risk; it does not guarantee fit.\n\nAnother misreading is to merge the two outputs into one smooth paragraph and call that the deliverable. The deliverable is the log, not the blended text. A reviewer needs to see the disagreement and your tie-break, because that record, not the polished sentence, is the evidence that you reasoned rather than copied.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake a resume summary line and walk the workflow end to end. Draft: \"Detail-oriented student seeking opportunities to grow.\" Paste it into Tool A, which says \"too generic, lead with a concrete skill,\" and into Tool B, which says \"good, maybe add the word passionate.\" Sort the critiques: this is a conflict, A wants substance, B wants an adjective. Go to the posting, which asks for \"experience with data analysis.\" That external anchor settles it toward A and against B, because adding \"passionate\" answers nothing the posting requested.\n\nNow act and log. Revised line: \"Statistics minor with hands-on Excel and SQL data-analysis experience, seeking an analyst internship.\" Log entry: accepted A (lead with concrete skill, anchored by the posting's data-analysis requirement); rejected B (the adjective adds no checkable substance). The walkthrough shows the pattern in miniature: capture both critiques, find the conflict, break the tie with the posting, then write a log row that a reviewer could audit without re-running the tools.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader notices that the interesting information is in the disagreements and goes looking for them on purpose, rather than skimming for whatever both tools praised. They treat a conflict as a question handed back to them, not as a defect in the tools.\n\nA strong reader also keeps the tools in their lane. The tools are good at surface issues (wordiness, weak verbs, formatting), and unreliable about facts (whether your GPA is real, whether you actually led a team). Knowing which advice to weigh and which to verify yourself is what separates using the workflow from being used by it.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same triangulation habit transfers any time you face two confident automated opinions. Comparing two AI explanations of a statistics problem, two grammar-checker rewrites of an email, or two navigation apps' routes all reward the same move: locate the conflict, then resolve it with an external anchor (the textbook, the assignment rules, the actual traffic) rather than the tool's tone. The anchor changes with the task, but the discipline of refusing to let a tool break its own tie is what you are really practicing here.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-02-two-ai-critique-workflow-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 02.02. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow", "cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"], "stem": "In a two-AI critique workflow you ask Tool A and Tool B to review the same cover letter. What is the point of using two tools instead of one?", "options": ["To get twice as many edits applied automatically so the letter is finished faster.", "To compare where the two tools agree and disagree, then use the disagreements as prompts for your own judgment.", "To average the two outputs into a single rewrite.", "To pick whichever tool writes in a more formal tone."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Two tools are useful precisely because they differ. Agreement flags likely-real issues; disagreement is where you must decide, which is the judgment the assignment is testing.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_more_tools_more_edits", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Tool A says your resume summary is \"too generic.\" Tool B says it is \"clear and concise.\" The two tools conflict. What is the strongest next move?", "options": ["Side with Tool B because positive feedback means the summary is done.", "Side with Tool A because negative feedback is always more useful.", "Re-read the summary against the specific job posting, decide which critique fits this audience, and log the reason for your choice.", "Run a third tool and go with the majority vote."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Neither tool knows your target job. A conflict is a signal to apply your own criterion (fit to the posting) and record why you accepted one critique over the other.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_tie_breaking_by_tone", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow"], "stem": "Both AI tools recommend the exact same change: \"Add a quantified achievement to the first bullet.\" You have no real number to add. What should your critique log show?", "options": ["Accepted: invented a plausible number since both tools agreed.", "Rejected: both tools flagged a real gap, but I cannot supply a truthful number, so I noted the gap and will seek a real metric before adding one.", "Ignored: agreement between tools is just coincidence.", "Accepted: added the words \"significantly improved\" instead of a number."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Agreement between tools usually points to a real weakness, so do not dismiss it. But the fix must be truthful. The honest log records the valid critique and the reason you could not yet act on it.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_agreement_forces_action", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_two_ai_critique_workflow_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat100_supplement_two_ai_critique_workflow"], "stem": "Which critique-log entry best documents one round of the two-AI workflow?", "options": ["\"Used AI to fix my resume.\"", "\"Tool A: shorten summary. Tool B: keep summary. Decision: shortened from 4 lines to 2 because the posting asks for brevity. Rejected Tool B's 'keep as is.'\"", "\"Both tools made it better.\"", "\"AI gave me lots of good ideas.\""], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A usable log names each tool's suggestion, the decision, the concrete change (4 lines to 2), the reason (posting asks for brevity), and what was rejected. The other options record no traceable judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_vague_log_entry", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_more_tools_more_edits", "pattern": "more_tools_means_more_edits", "description": "Thinking the value of two AI tools is doubling the number of automatic edits rather than surfacing disagreement", "trigger": "learner applies every edit from both tools without comparing them", "feedback": "The point of two tools is the comparison. Look at where they agree and disagree, and treat disagreement as a decision you must make.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_tie_breaking_by_tone", "pattern": "resolving_conflict_by_tone", "description": "Resolving a conflict between two tools by trusting whichever sounds more positive or more confident", "trigger": "learner picks the friendlier or more authoritative critique instead of checking it against the goal", "feedback": "Neither tool knows your target audience. Resolve conflicts against the job posting or rubric, not against tone.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_agreement_forces_action", "pattern": "agreement_forces_untruthful_fix", "description": "Assuming that when both tools agree, you must apply the fix even if it means inventing information", "trigger": "learner adds a fabricated metric because both tools requested one", "feedback": "Agreement signals a real gap, but the fix must stay truthful. Log the valid critique and find a real number before adding one.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_2_2_vague_log_entry", "pattern": "untraceable_critique_log", "description": "Writing a critique log that does not name each tool's suggestion, the decision, or the concrete change", "trigger": "learner records \"used AI to fix i